{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>But only for his close flesh, etc.</b> — It is taught in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״שְׁאֵרוֹ״ — this is his wife, as it is written ״שְׁאֵר אָבִיךָ״. ״הַקָּרֹב״ — to exclude the betrothed (arusah), meaning, for the side that she has not yet become close. ״אֵלָיו״ — to exclude the divorced woman, meaning, one who was close and has become distant. If it says ״לְאִמּוֹ,״ what does it need to say ״לְאָבִיו״? Just as his mother, who becomes profaned (mitchalelet), he nonetheless defiles himself for her — his father, who does not become profaned, is it not logical (kal va-chomer) that he should defile himself for him? If so, I would say: just as his mother is certain (yadu'a), so too his father is certain. From where do we know his father even by presumption (chezkat)? It is derived by saying ״וּלְאָבִיו״ — thus far. The sages of Lunel raised a difficulty to Rabbi Yitzchak: The verse would never say ״לְאָבִיו״ and we would derive it by kal va-chomer from his mother. What would you say? Behold, this father is not as certain as his mother — he should defile himself regardless: if this is his father — he defiles himself, and if he is not his father, if so he is a mamzer and has no priesthood — thus far." "And in my eyes this is no difficulty at all, for the verse here comes for two things: to permit and to obligate. To permit defilement for close relatives, even though he prohibited it for distant relatives; and to obligate — that if he did not wish to defile himself, they defile him against his will, as the verse said ״לָהּ יִטַּמָּא״ and they said in Torat Kohanim ״מִצְוָה״ — that every priest is obligated to defile himself for the dead mentioned in the matter. And although ״לָהּ יִטַּמָּא״ was said only regarding his sister, behold they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: There was an incident involving Yosef the kohen, whose wife died on the eve of Passover and he did not wish to defile himself for her, and the sages pushed him and defiled him against his will — thus far is their language. Behold, it is set before you that for all the close relatives mentioned in the matter he is obligated to defile himself. Accordingly, if I were to judge the defilement for his father by the logic of ״מָה נַפְשָׁךְ״ (regardless:), I would have no grounds to obligate him to defile himself for him, for he would say: who says this is his father? And moreover, if his father dies on the eve of Passover, one cannot nullify the commandment of Passover at its appointed time on account of a doubt. And no difficulty arises according to the one who says (Rambam and Pri Chadash) that a Torah-level doubt is permitted by Torah law — it is obvious that he would not defile himself to push off the commandment of Passover at its appointed time. But even according to the one who says that a Torah-level doubt is forbidden by Torah law, even so one should not push off a certain commandment on account of a doubtful commandment. Therefore it was necessary to say ״לְאִמּוֹ וּלְאָבִיו״ — that he is obligated to defile himself for each of them by Torah law. And see the verse that follows this one." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it appears to me, since there exists a situation where his father is known only by presumption (chezkah), and the son has the status of a kohen — for example, when we have before us two men, each independently presumed to be a kohen, and also presumed that one is the father and one the son — and this situation could be one where the presumption of the father that he is a kohen is undermined but the presumption of the son is not undermined to remove him from the rank of kohen, and we would say that this one is not his father and the son remains in his presumption. And since this is so, if it had not said ״לְאָבִיו״ I would have ruled that he should not defile himself for his father by presumption in the manner described, for the reason that perhaps the presumption of his father is undermined and the son remains a kohen forever. Therefore the Tanna said: From where do we know his father even by presumption? It is derived by saying ״לְאָבִיו.״ And behold, whether according to this approach or according to the first approach, his father by presumption — he is obligated to defile himself for him, for after all his father is nothing but a presumption, and the Torah obligated him to defile himself." "<b>And for his son and for his daughter and for his brother, etc.</b> It is taught in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: If it says ״לִבְנוֹ וּלְבִתּוֹ,״ what does it need to say ״לְאִמּוֹ וּלְאָבִיו״? His son and his daughter, whom he is not obligated to honor — behold he defiles himself for them; his father and his mother, whom he is obligated to honor — is it not logical that he should defile himself for them? If so, I would say: for his son and his daughter who are stillborn (nefalim). It is derived by saying ״לְאִמּוֹ וּלְאָבִיו״ — just as his mother and father are viable (benei kayyama), so too his son and daughter are viable — thus far. It is difficult: accordingly, it should have needed to say only ״לְאִמּוֹ וְלִבְנוֹ״ etc., and not say ״לְאָבִיו.״ And as for the claim of ״מָה אִמּוֹ בְּיָדוּעַ״ (just as his mother is certain), his son and daughter will prove the point, for presumably they are not certain but only by presumption, yet he defiles himself for them. And one cannot say that his father is weaker (i.e., less certain) than his son and daughter, for behold we find that the Tanna derives a kal va-chomer from them to his father and mother — if so, why was it necessary to say ״לְאָבִיו״? And if it is to reveal about his son and daughter that they must be viable — we derive that from the extra word ״לְאִמּוֹ,״ which should be unnecessary, since it already comes by kal va-chomer from son and daughter. And one cannot say that both father and mother together are needed to exclude stillborn sons and daughters, for why would one not suffice? And furthermore, if both are needed, what room is there for the Tanna to make his derivations between father and mother — are not both needed to exclude stillborn sons and daughters? And the matter is straightforward." "<b>And it appears</b> to say, that if it had not said ״לְאָבִיו״ I would have reasoned along this line: what does it need to say ״לְאִמּוֹ״? His son and his daughter, whom he is not obligated to honor, he defiles himself for them — his mother, all the more so! If so, I would have reasoned the same for his father as well. It is derived by saying ״לְאִמּוֹ״ — to exclude his father, for the reason that he is nothing but a presumption. And we would have been compelled to explain that the son and daughter the verse speaks of are similar to his mother in being certain (yadu'a), in a scenario where his wife could have conceived only from him, by means of clear knowledge in a situation that could exist. But now that the verse said ״לְאָבִיו״ and we asked why it was necessary, and his law would be derived from his mother — we are forced to say that it comes to tell us even if he is only a presumption. If so, why do I need ״אִמּוֹ״? Learn from it to exclude stillborn sons and daughters. And although the Tanna said ״לְאָבִיו וּלְאִמּוֹ״ to exclude the stillborn — this is not precise (lav davka), and he relied on what he drew attention to in the adjacent derivation of ״לְאָבִיו,״ which comes to say even if he is only a presumption." "<b>Rather,</b> it is difficult why they said there in Torat Kohanim in the latter part of the baraita of ״לְאִמּוֹ וּלְאָבִיו״, and this is their language: If it had said ״לְאָבִיו״ and not ״לְאִמּוֹ״ etc., I would say: just as his father, who does not become profaned, so too his mother, who does not become profaned — from where do I derive his mother who has become profaned (חללה)? Scripture says ״לְאִמּוֹ״ — end of quote. We see that ״לְאִמּוֹ״ was needed to include even one who has become profaned. And from where does he derive to exclude the stillborn (נפלים)? And if you interpret it for the stillborn, then one who has become profaned — from where? Furthermore, one needs to know accordingly, regarding his daughter who has become profaned, whether he is obligated to defile himself for her or not, for there are grounds to say specifically his mother, whom he is obligated to honor, is the one who was included even if she became profaned, and there are grounds to say since Scripture revealed that profanation does not invalidate the closeness, the same law applies to his daughter." "<b>However,</b> one must question the words of the Tanna: from where did he find to say that he defiles himself for his mother who has become profaned? If from the superfluity, perhaps it was needed because I would have said ״לְאָבִיו״ on account of the fact that he has a quality that he does not become profaned, unlike the mother, who is inferior to the father in that she does become profaned. And so the Tanna meticulously phrased at the beginning of his words, saying ״מָה אָבִיו שֶׁאֵינוֹ מִתְחַלֵּל״ — implying that the distinction would require making a gradation in him, even though it is not found in reality but only since it is fit to be so, and if so, we may say until now that for his mother who has become profaned he is exempt from defiling himself. And it is compelling to say that the Tanna holds that Scripture's saying ״לְאִמּוֹ״ in its plain meaning includes whether she is a priest's wife or a profaned woman (חללה). The truth is that if Scripture had said only ״לְאָבִיו״, the opponent could have objected and said: his mother is inferior to his father even when she is a priestess, since she is subject to profanation; but after it said ״לְאִמּוֹ״ — even if she became profaned is included in the meaning. And likewise you would deduce when Scripture says ״וּלְבִתּוֹ״ — even if she became profaned is included in the meaning, for Scripture did not condition the matter on anything other than the cause of kinship, and his mother who has become profaned and his daughter who has become profaned are still called his mother and his daughter. From this point on, since even without an explicit inclusion we say that the meaning of ״בִּתּוֹ״ includes even one who became profaned — anyone called his daughter he defiles himself for — if so, we come to ask: why did it say ״אִמּוֹ״? For it would come through a fortiori inference from his daughter, and if his daughter who became profaned — he defiles himself for her, is his mother needed?" "<b>However,</b> if Scripture had not said ״לְאִמּוֹ״, then we would not have said that ״לְאָבִיו״ comes to exclude his stillborn sons and daughters, but rather to exclude his mother. And this exclusion is more plausible to hear than to go searching for the stillborn of his sons and daughters to say that it was on their account. And I would have excluded his mother on the grounds that she becomes profaned. And when we would come to reason from his daughter who becomes profaned and he defiles himself for her, we would have established the exclusion as applying specifically to his mother when she has become profaned, but when she has not become profaned, she would come through a fortiori inference from his daughter. And when we come to reason regarding his daughter even if she became profaned from the meaning of the term as we said above — that even one who became profaned is called his daughter — we would then turn back and reason about his daughter who became profaned from his mother who became profaned: and if his mother who became profaned — he does not defile himself for her, as we derived from the superfluity of ״לְאָבִיו״ — then his daughter who became profaned, all the more so. And even though the meaning of ״בִּתּוֹ״ indicates even one who became profaned, that is from the plain sense; but when a hermeneutical derivation through exclusion or a fortiori inference comes, we revert to restricting it to one who has not become profaned." "<b>What emerges</b> from all of this in the depth of Scripture is: if Scripture had not said ״לְאָבִיו״ — from which we would come to the error of saying it comes to exclude his mother as I have written — it would not have needed to say ״לְאִמּוֹ״, and she would have been derived from his son and daughter as mentioned. And if it had not said ״לְאִמּוֹ״ — from which the error would have arisen to say it comes to exclude his father on account of the fact that he is known only by presumption (חֲזָקָה) — it would not have needed to say ״לְאָבִיו״. If so, it would be difficult: why not say neither his father nor his mother? Therefore they derived that it comes to exclude the stillborn, that he does not defile himself for them. And from this point on, there is no room for us to say that it should state either ״לְאִמּוֹ״ alone or ״לְאָבִיו״ alone, for whichever one it might say would give rise to the error and demolish the structure. And the blessed HaShem was wise to speak words of truth, and therefore the Tanna was wise and first informed us of the error that arises if only one of them is stated, and afterward derived the superfluity of both of them together — and understand this. And through this, the words of the Tanna ascend as an eternal light, the words of the living El."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The parenthetical attribution to Rambam and Pri Chadash appears in the source text.", 5 "The baraita cited is from Sifra, the halakhic midrash on Leviticus (referred to here as Torat Kohanim). I rendered מִתְחַלֵּל / חִלּוּל with the root 'profane' throughout to preserve the connection to חֲלָלָה (a profaned/halal person).", 6 "Reading מַשְׁמָעוּת as 'plain meaning / implications of the term' throughout. The word הַהַדְרָגָה (gradation) is a technical logical term here; I rendered it literally.", 7 "The parenthetical (כְּמוֹ שֶׁדָּרַשְׁנוּ מִיִּתּוּר '״לְאָבִיו״') refers back to the hypothetical exclusion of the mother derived in this same comment; I translated the parenthetical in situ.", 8 "Reading יִסְתֹּר הַבִּנְיָן as 'demolish the structure' (i.e., the logical construction of the halakhic derivation). The final phrase דִּבְרֵי אֱלֹהִים חַיִּים is a standard doxology; rendered literally as 'words of the living El.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:03.090184360Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}